# modules/sast/rules/nosql.rules - docs/DESIGN.md §6.3 "nosql.rules ... NoSQL
# query operators built from user input (e.g. $where, object injection)" seed
# catalog: the server-side JavaScript forms ($where, $function/$accumulator,
# mapReduce), operator injection through a request-derived object, and a query
# document assembled as text and parsed back.
#
# The catalog names MongoDB's own vocabulary and nothing else, so this pack
# covers the MongoDB drivers for the three languages the shipped packs already
# cover - the Node driver/Mongoose, PyMongo, and the Java driver.  No new
# language is introduced here, and no other datastore's query language is
# guessed at: a rule for a driver this repository has no fixture for would be a
# check nothing measures.
#
# `db.eval(` is deliberately NOT re-declared under a SAST-NOSQL-* id.  Its
# bytes already match SAST-JS-EVAL-01 (modules/sast/rules/javascript.rules) in
# a .js file and SAST-PY-EVAL_EXEC-01 (modules/sast/rules/python.rules) in a
# .py file, because both patterns anchor on `\beval\s*\(` and the `.` before
# `eval` is a word boundary.  Minting a third id for the same bytes would fire
# two check ids on one line for one root cause - the precedent java.rules set
# for Runtime.exec and javascript.rules set for child_process.exec.
#
# Every `files` glob is written as a plain extension pattern (`*.py`), never
# `**/*.py`.  Per rules/RULE-FORMAT.md §9.1.2 an unanchored glob already
# matches at any depth, whereas `**/` requires at least one directory
# component and so silently MISSES a file at the scan root - invisible in a
# fixture tree, where every path is nested by construction.
#
# Unlike modules/sast/rules/injection.rules, which is deliberately unscoped
# because its sinks are language-agnostic, this pack carries explicit globs.
# tests/fixtures/{vuln,clean}/ are SHARED trees scanned wholesale, and the
# query-construction vocabulary here (a `{` … `+` run, a `.find(` call) is
# common enough in YAML, JSON, and Terraform fixtures that an unscoped pack
# would be a cross-fire waiting to happen.  The boundary against
# modules/sast/rules/ldap.rules is the subject matter itself and is asserted in
# both directions in tests/suites/sast.sh: no NoSQL check may attach to an LDAP
# fixture, and no LDAP check to a NoSQL one.
#
# SAST-NOSQL-OPERATOR_INJECTION-01 carries no `context-deny` for a coercion
# helper, and that is deliberate.  A deny wide enough to see a String()/str()
# call written a line earlier is exactly the false negative
# rules/RULE-FORMAT.md §10.2 and docs/FOUNDATION.md finding F4 forbid, and a
# same-line deny would suppress a genuinely vulnerable filter the moment one of
# its OTHER fields happened to be coerced.  The safe equivalent is therefore a
# genuinely different shape - coerce or validate the value into a scalar first,
# then bind that scalar into the query document - which is what this pack's
# clean fixtures use and what the remediation asks for.

id: SAST-NOSQL-WHERE_JS-01
title: MongoDB $where expression built from concatenated or interpolated input
severity: critical
confidence: medium
cwe: CWE-95
owasp: A05:2025
pattern: \$where["']?[[:space:]]*[:,].*(["'][[:space:]]*\+|\$\{|\.format\(|%[[:space:]]*[A-Za-z_(])
dialect: ere
files: *.js
files: *.jsx
files: *.ts
files: *.tsx
files: *.mjs
files: *.cjs
files: *.py
files: *.java
tags: static
tags: quick
remediation: Express the condition with ordinary query operators ($eq, $gt, $regex with a
  bound value) so the supplied value is compared as data. A $where clause is a JavaScript
  expression the database server evaluates, so any value concatenated or interpolated into
  it is executed, not compared. If the logic genuinely cannot be expressed without it,
  disable server-side scripting instead and move the comparison into the application.
references: CWE-95
references: CWE-943

id: SAST-NOSQL-SERVER_JS-01
title: Server-side JavaScript evaluation requested from the database
severity: medium
confidence: low
cwe: CWE-94
owasp: A05:2025
pattern: (\$function["']?[[:space:]]*[:,]|\$accumulator["']?[[:space:]]*[:,]|\.(mapReduce|map_reduce)[[:space:]]*\()
dialect: ere
files: *.js
files: *.jsx
files: *.ts
files: *.tsx
files: *.mjs
files: *.cjs
files: *.py
files: *.java
tags: static
severity-floor: low
remediation: Replace the JavaScript body with the aggregation pipeline's own operators,
  which the server evaluates natively and cannot be made to execute a caller's code.
  Every one of these features hands a JavaScript function to the database engine, so the
  whole function body becomes a code-injection sink the moment any part of it is built
  from data, and the feature is worth disabling on the server outright once nothing needs
  it.
references: CWE-94

id: SAST-NOSQL-OPERATOR_INJECTION-01
title: Request-derived value used directly inside a NoSQL query document
severity: high
confidence: medium
cwe: CWE-943
owasp: A05:2025
pattern: \.(find_one_and_update|find_one_and_delete|findOneAndUpdate|findOneAndDelete|find_one|findOne|delete_one|deleteOne|delete_many|deleteMany|update_one|updateOne|update_many|updateMany|count_documents|countDocuments|aggregate|find)[[:space:]]*\([[:space:]]*(\{[^}]*)?(req|request)\.(body|query|params|json|args|form)
dialect: ere
files: *.js
files: *.jsx
files: *.ts
files: *.tsx
files: *.mjs
files: *.cjs
files: *.py
files: *.java
tags: static
tags: quick
remediation: Coerce or validate each request value into the scalar the field expects
  before it reaches the query document, and bind that scalar rather than the raw value.
  A request body or query string can decode to an object, so a caller who sends an
  operator where a string was expected rewrites the filter itself - the shape that turns
  a login lookup into an unconditional match.
references: CWE-943

id: SAST-NOSQL-QUERY_CONCAT-01
title: NoSQL query document parsed from a concatenated string
severity: high
confidence: medium
cwe: CWE-943
owasp: A05:2025
pattern: (JSON\.parse|json\.loads|Document\.parse|BasicDBObject\.parse|BsonDocument\.parse)[[:space:]]*\([^)]*\{[^)]*\+
dialect: ere
files: *.js
files: *.jsx
files: *.ts
files: *.tsx
files: *.mjs
files: *.cjs
files: *.py
files: *.java
tags: static
remediation: Build the query as a document - an object literal, or the driver's own filter
  builders - with each value bound as data, instead of assembling query text and parsing
  it back. Concatenating into a document's serialized form lets a supplied value close its
  string and add fields or operators of its own, which is the same weakness as
  concatenating SQL text.
references: CWE-943
